OpenAPI v1 Contract Design with BYOK Model
1. Business Perspective
Infrastructure Copilot must support enterprises that already have:

Their own Azure OpenAI subscription
Their own OpenAI Enterprise account
Their own Anthropic account
Their own AWS Bedrock account
Their own GCP Vertex AI setup
Their own security rules
Their own compliance requirements
Their own cost centers

So we design Infrastructure Copilot as:

A SaaS orchestration, investigation, RAG, and governance platform where the customer can bring their own AI provider credentials, model endpoint, or keyless identity integration.

This gives us three commercial advantages:

Lower SaaS operating cost because model inference cost can sit with the customer.
Better enterprise trust because customers control their AI provider and data plane.
Faster security approval because customers can use their existing cloud governance.

Important distinction:





















TermMeaning for Infrastructure CopilotBYOK for AI providerCustomer brings model provider credentials, endpoint, deployment name, or identity configurationCMK/BYOK for encryptionCustomer brings encryption keys through Azure Key Vault or cloud KMSKeyless modelCustomer authorizes Infrastructure Copilot through Entra ID / managed identity / workload identity instead of sharing static API keys
Microsoft documentation says Azure OpenAI supports customer-managed keys, also called Bring Your Own Key, through Azure Key Vault for encryption control.  Microsoft also documents keyless Azure OpenAI access through Microsoft Entra ID and managed identity, noting that keyless authentication avoids storing account keys or connection strings. [learn.microsoft.com] [learn.microsoft.com]
My recommendation: BYOK must support both API-key mode and keyless mode, but keyless should be the enterprise-preferred path.

