BYOK Architecture Decision
Wrong BYOK Design
Plain Text1Customer pastes API key2↓3We store plaintext key4↓5We call model directlyShow more lines
No. Absolutely not. That is how you build a future breach notification.
Correct BYOK Design
Plain Text1Customer configures AI provider2↓3Infrastructure Copilot stores metadata in PostgreSQL4↓5Secret value is stored in Key Vault or customer vault reference6↓7AI Gateway resolves provider at runtime8↓9Request is routed through tenant-scoped model configuration10↓11Usage is logged per tenantShow more lines
Azure Key Vault guidance says secrets are intended for sensitive application credentials such as API keys, passwords, connection strings, and access keys.  Microsoft also recommends managed identities as the best way to authenticate to Azure services when possible, while secrets require rotation when managed identity is not an option. [learn.microsoft.com] [microsoft.github.io]

BYOK Modes
Mode 1: Customer-Provided API Key
Used for:
Plain Text1OpenAI2Anthropic3Custom LLM gateway4Some Azure OpenAI setupsShow more lines
We store:
Plain Text1provider_type2endpoint_url3deployment_name4secret_reference5model_capabilitiesShow more lines
We do not store:
Plain Text1raw_api_keyShow more lines

Mode 2: Azure OpenAI Keyless
Used for enterprise Azure customers.
Customer grants access using:
Plain Text1Microsoft Entra ID2Managed Identity3RBACShow more lines
Microsoft documents that Azure OpenAI can use Microsoft Entra ID authentication and role assignments such as Cognitive Services OpenAI User or Contributor instead of key-based authentication. [learn.microsoft.com]

Mode 3: Customer-Managed Encryption Key
Used for encryption governance.
Azure OpenAI documentation says customer-managed keys provide flexibility to create, rotate, disable, revoke access controls, and audit encryption keys.  Azure AI Services customer-managed key guidance says the key vault must enable soft delete and purge protection, and the managed identity must have key permissions such as wrap key, unwrap key, and get. [learn.microsoft.com] [learn.microsoft.com]

3. Security Considerations
The internal Microsoft-Secure-AI--Technical-Guide-for-Architects frames secure AI as an enterprise control-plane problem involving identity, data governance, cloud posture, application security, policy enforcement, detection engineering, compliance, and operational resilience.  It also warns that AI systems can invoke identity tokens, query indexed content, retrieve enterprise data, call connectors, interact with APIs, and trigger agent behavior in one transaction, which is why identity governance and control boundaries matter. [Microsoft...Architects | SharePoint] [Microsoft...Architects | SharePoint]
Mandatory Security Rules

Never expose provider keys to frontend.
Never store raw API keys in PostgreSQL.
Store only secret_reference.
Support customer-owned vault references later.
Use tenant-scoped provider configuration.
Every AI call must be logged.
Every model response must be associated with tenant, user, provider, model, token usage, and cost.
BYOK validation must test connectivity without storing plaintext secrets.
Key rotation must be supported without downtime.
Provider configuration changes must generate audit events.

Azure Key Vault guidance recommends rotating secrets regularly and using tags for metadata such as rotation schedules, expiration dates, and ownership. [learn.microsoft.com]

4. Scalability
BYOK improves SaaS scalability because:

Inference load can be distributed across customer-owned model providers.
Enterprise customers can use their own rate limits and quotas.
We avoid one shared OpenAI quota becoming the bottleneck.
Customers can choose regional deployments.
MSPs can map different customers to different model providers.

But we still need a Model Gateway.
